'''Fosian Liba''', also known as '''North Fosian Liba''', and historically referred to as '''Dagiti Liba''' (from the Dagiti demonym for the [[Republic of Dagit|Dagit]] people, meaning "free Dagiti") or '''Dagiti Balboan people''', are a multigenerational community in [[Balboa]] descended from peoples from across the North Fosian Peninsula who escaped slavery in [[Sinchew]] during the [[The Secret Trade|Secret Trade]] era (16th–18th centuries) and found refuge in colonial Balboa. The escapees were drawn primarily from the [[Balausia Peninsula]] (modern-day [[Balausia]], [[Pangkajene]], and [[Tondano]]) and the [[Dagit]] archipelago, at a time when the modern concept of a unified "Dagit" identity did not yet exist.

The term ''Dagiti Liba'' was adopted by the community in the 1950s and formalised through the [[Twelve Guarantees]] framework, which provisioned special rights to recognised minority ethnic groups. Before the 1950s, most people of North Fosian descent in Balboa were regarded as part of the [[Mixed Balboan people|Mixed Balboan]] population without a distinct communal identity. Since the 2010s, ''Dagiti Liba'' has been increasingly regarded as archaic, with community members and scholars shifting to ''Fosian Liba'' or ''North Fosian Liba'' — terms that better reflect the community's diverse origins across the peninsula rather than solely the modern Republic of Dagit.

The community is the oldest non-Balboan community in the country. A small but distinct Fosian Liba culture persists, concentrated primarily in [[Migjorn Province]] with a smaller presence in [[Estret Province]].

== History ==

=== The Secret Trade and enslavement ===

[[File:Dagit escape route Secret Trade.png|thumb|250px|Escape routes from Sinchew to colonial Balboa during the Secret Trade era]]

The community traces its origins to the [[The Secret Trade|Secret Trade]], a system of slave trading that operated in the Zong colony of [[Sinchew]] from the late sixteenth century through the mid-eighteenth century. Pirates and mercenaries kidnapped peoples from across the North Fosian Peninsula — primarily the [[Balausia Peninsula]] (modern-day [[Balausia]], [[Pangkajene]], and [[Tondano]]) and the [[Dagit]] archipelago — and sold them as chattel slaves to Zong planters in Sinchew. The enslaved population was put to work on sugar, indigo, and other tropical crop plantations.

At the time, the modern concept of "Dagit" as a unified national identity did not exist; the peoples of the North Fosian Peninsula were organised into a constellation of polities, sultanates, and communities. The [[Zong Edict of 1500]] banned slavery on the mainland, but its applicability to overseas colonies was legally ambiguous, allowing the trade to persist for over a century until Zong naval action in 1680 and the transition to a self-reproducing enslaved population gradually suppressed large-scale raiding.

=== Escape to colonial Balboa ===

From the earliest years of the Secret Trade, enslaved North Fosian people sought to escape to the Volisanian colony of Balboa. Two routes were available:

* '''Sea route''' (more common): followed the lanes between Sinchew and Balboa, aided by Fosian sailors put to work on coastal vessels and networks of free Fosian and mixed-race mariners. The maritime traditions of the North Fosian peoples made this route viable, though it was subject to storms, naval patrols, and recapture.
* '''Land route''': through the Lurandian isthmus, using dense jungle and mountainous terrain as cover. Longer and more gruelling, exposed to disease and hostile groups, but used by those held on inland plantations far from the coast.

Under Volisanian colonial law, Fosian peoples were classified outside the colonial slavery hierarchy, which was structured around the enslavement of Kaftian peoples. This meant escaped Fosian slaves could not be readily re-enslaved in Balboa, though they enjoyed no formal protections and faced poverty, discrimination, and social marginalisation. Most settled among the poor free Black and Mixed populations of the colonial towns — particularly [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Portnou]], and the rural communities of [[Migjorn Province|Migjorn]], where the remote coast provided favourable conditions for permanent settlement.

=== Intermixing and cultural persistence ===

Over the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, the majority of North Fosian descendants intermixed with the broader Black and Mixed Balboan populations. Intermarriage with free Black and Mixed Balboans — who shared similar economic circumstances — was common, and most descendants were absorbed into the Mixed Balboan population, losing knowledge of their specific Fosian heritage. By the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, they were generally regarded — both by themselves and by wider Balboan society — as part of the Mixed Balboan population.

A minority of descendants, particularly in the isolated coastal communities of Migjorn, maintained fragments of cultural practice — maritime traditions, Anitist religious elements, and linguistic features. These preserved practices were a hybrid blend drawn from across the North Fosian Peninsula, reflecting the diverse origins of the escapee population and centuries of creolisation. The communities preserved elements of Balausian, Pangkajene, Tondano, and other North Fosian language, religion, and maritime tradition in intermingled form, adapting them to the Balboan context. The specific peninsular sources of these practices were no longer distinguishable.

=== Origins of the term "Dagiti Liba" ===

The term ''Dagiti Liba'' ("free Dagiti") is not a historical name continuous with the escape era. It is a self-referential term that emerged in the 1950s, catalysed by two developments:

The first was the migration of Dagiti labourers to the [[International City of Areza|Areza]] construction project in the early 1950s. Among the labourers were recent immigrants from the [[Republic of Dagit]] — which had existed as a unified nation only since the [[Dagiti Revolution of 1850]] — who encountered the older descendant communities in Migjorn and Sant Cristòfor. The similarities between the immigrants' traditions and the descendants' attenuated practices — the Anitist religious framework, maritime customs, and fragments of shared vocabulary — suggested a common North Fosian origin. However, the descendants' culture was not a preserved Dagit tradition but a distinct creolised hybrid drawn from across the peninsula. The recognition that the community had a specific hybrid culture, rather than a degraded form of Dagit culture, was a key factor in the movement to give the community a name. ''Dagiti'' was adopted as the most familiar North Fosian reference point, even though the community's heritage was broader.

The second was the [[Twelve Guarantees]] framework (1960), which provisioned special rights and protections for recognised minority ethnic groups. The Guarantees initially recognised only the larger Fosian communities — Zong, Sinchew, and Nakamian — but created an incentive for smaller communities to articulate a distinct ethnic identity. Community organisers adopted ''Dagiti Liba'' in the 1950s and early 1960s, using it in the petitions and lobbying that eventually led to formal recognition as a protected Fosian cultural group in 1978.

''Dagiti'' is the demonym for the people of the [[Republic of Dagit]] — analogous to "Volisanian" for Volisania or "Balausian" for Balausia — and as a national designation dates only to the mid-nineteenth century. The escapees of the Secret Trade era predated this identity, and the application of the demonym to their descendants is a retrospective projection of a modern national category onto a pre-national population. This anachronism is a primary reason the term has been increasingly regarded as archaic.

=== Post-reunification recognition ===

[[File:Dagiti recognition movement 1970s Sant Cristofor.png|thumb|250px|Dagiti Liba community organisers during the recognition movement, Sant Cristòfor, c. 1975]]

The [[Twelve Guarantees]] (1960) initially excluded the Fosian Liba, reflecting the community's small size, eroding distinct identity, and the perception among Balboan officials that they were simply part of the Mixed Balboan population. In the 1970s, a recognition movement emerged, led by community organisers in Migjorn and Sant Cristòfor who argued that the community met the criteria for protected Fosian status: a distinct language, a distinct religion (Anitism), and a documented history of Fosian origin. After sustained advocacy, the community was formally recognised as a protected Fosian cultural group in 1978. The recognition came late relative to the larger Fosian communities, and the Guarantees have slowed but not reversed the community's assimilation.

=== University of Sant Cristòfor ethnic study (2000s) ===

In the early 2000s, the [[University of Sant Cristòfor]] conducted a comprehensive ethnic study of the Fosian Liba community, funded by the Adonian Community. The study collected oral histories, genealogical records, and DNA samples from community members across Migjorn and Estret provinces.

The findings significantly complicated the community's understanding of its origins. While the term ''Dagiti Liba'' presupposed a specific connection to the Republic of Dagit, the study found that most community members' heritage traced to populations across the [[Balausia Peninsula]] — including modern-day [[Balausia]], [[Pangkajene]], and [[Tondano]] — with a smaller proportion from the Dagit archipelago. The community's archaic linguistic variety, while most closely related to pre-galleon-trade Dagit, also contained features consistent with a broader North Fosian linguistic continuum, including elements shared with languages of the Balausia Peninsula. The study concluded that the escapee population represented a cross-section of North Fosian peoples, not exclusively from Dagit.

=== Shift in terminology (2010s) ===

The University of Sant Cristòfor study, combined with the recognition that ''Dagiti Liba'' rested on a retrospective projection of a modern demonym onto a pre-national population, prompted a shift in terminology. By the 2010s, community organisations, academic publications, and government documents began adopting '''North Fosian Liba''' or '''Fosian Liba''' — terms that more accurately reflect the community's diverse origins.

The shift has not been universal. Older community members, particularly in Migjorn, continue to use ''Dagiti Liba'', which retains cultural significance as the name under which the community organised and achieved recognition. The 1978 recognition was granted under the name ''Dagiti Liba'', and the legal framework associated with the Guarantees continues to use this designation.

== Population and distribution ==

[[File:Dagiti Balboan fishing village Migjorn.png|thumb|250px|A Fosian Liba fishing village on the Migjorn coast]]

The Fosian Liba population is estimated at approximately 52,000:

