The cultural gulf is a source of mutual tension. The Fosian Liba regard the Nou-Dagiti as culturally foreign newcomers with no connection to their history of suffering and survival. The Nou-Dagiti, in turn, often view the Fosian Liba as assimilated Balboans who have lost the language and traditions that define Dagiti identity. The two communities live in different areas, attend different religious institutions, and maintain separate community organisations.

== Culture ==

=== Language ===

The [[Dagiti language|Dagiti]] language (native name: ''Sang Dagiti'') is in severe decline among the Fosian Liba. The community's variety is an archaic, creolised form preserved from the Secret Trade era and isolated from the linguistic developments of the modern Dagit archipelago. It retains the indigenous Austronesian grammatical structure and core vocabulary — ''tubig'' (water), ''kaon'' (eat), ''balay'' (house), ''dagat'' (sea), ''anito'' (ancestor spirit) — but lacks the extensive Volisanian commercial superstrate that characterises modern Dagiti, which developed through the Cinc Parets–Macaio galleon trade. Instead, the Fosian Liba variety has absorbed Volisanian and Balboan Creole loanwords independently. The University of Sant Cristòfor study found that the variety also contains elements consistent with a broader North Fosian linguistic continuum, including features shared with languages of the Balausia Peninsula.

In Migjorn, the older creolised form survives among older speakers. Younger generations speak primarily Balboan Creole, with Dagiti surviving only in ceremonial and religious contexts — Anitist rituals, funeral rites, and community gatherings. The language is classified as critically endangered. In Estret Province, Dagiti is essentially extinct as a spoken tongue, surviving only in a handful of words and expressions. Some Dagiti loanwords have entered the local Balboan Creole, particularly in domains related to the sea, fishing, and Anitist religious practice.

=== Religion ===

[[File:Anitist shrine Migjorn.png|thumb|250px|An Anitist shrine in a Fosian Liba community, Migjorn]]

The community is predominantly [[Anitism|Anitist]], maintaining a traditional religion centred on the veneration of ancestral spirits (''anit'') and nature deities associated with the sea, land, and harvest. In the Balboan context, Anitist practice has been syncretised with elements of [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholicism]], particularly the veneration of saints, which some community members have identified with specific ''anit'' spirits. Anitist shrines are found in the Migjorn coastal communities and in Sant Llorenç and Ciutat de l'Estació.

A minority practise Petran Catholicism, particularly in Estret Province where assimilation is more advanced. Catholic community members often maintain a dual religious practice, attending Mass while also participating in Anitist ceremonies.

=== Maritime traditions ===

[[File:Blessing of the Boats Migjorn.png|thumb|250px|The Blessing of the Boats ceremony in a Migjorn fishing village]]

The community's maritime heritage — rooted in the seafaring traditions of the North Fosian Peninsula and the escape routes of the Secret Trade era — remains central to its cultural identity. Fishing is both an economic activity and a cultural practice, with techniques, boat-building methods, and navigational knowledge passed down through generations. The annual Blessing of the Boats ceremony, held in Migjorn coastal villages at the start of the fishing season, is the community's most important cultural event, combining Anitist rituals with communal feasting.

=== Cuisine ===

[[File:Dagiti Balboan smoked fish Migjorn.png|thumb|250px|Smoked fish with chilli-citrus marinade, a Fosian Liba speciality]]

Fosian Liba cuisine is a maritime cuisine reflecting the community's long association with the sea. Fish and seafood are central, prepared using techniques that blend North Fosian traditions with Balboan Creole cooking. Notable preparations include smoked fish in chilli and citrus marinades, fish stews thickened with root vegetables and plantains, and rice dishes seasoned with North Fosian spice blends. The cuisine is less well-known outside the community than the Nakamian Balboan fusion tradition, but has gained recognition in recent years as part of a broader interest in Balboan regional cooking.

